---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="How to use the journey" description="How to use JavaScript analogies without losing the exact Sui rule underneath them.">
  <section class="page-hero page-shell narrow-hero journey-guide-hero">
    <p class="section-kicker">When an analogy feels too neat</p>
    <h1>How to use the stories in this journey.</h1>
    <p>The backpack and trail make unfamiliar ideas easier to hold. They are memory aids, not a replacement for Sui’s object model. Here is how to keep the useful part without carrying a half-true rule forward.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="page-shell method-page-grid">
    <div class="method-rule"><span>01</span><h2>Use the JavaScript instinct</h2><p>Start with the programming model you already have. It gives the new Sui idea somewhere familiar to land.</p></div>
    <div class="method-rule"><span>02</span><h2>Make the prediction</h2><p>Choose an answer before the reveal. That makes your current mental model visible, including the part that may need changing.</p></div>
    <div class="method-rule"><span>03</span><h2>Notice where the story stops</h2><p>Read the “Sui difference” every time. That sentence is the boundary between a useful analogy and the actual rule.</p></div>
    <div class="method-rule"><span>04</span><h2>Check the evidence</h2><p>Return to the object, command, type, effect, or balance value underneath the explanation. The chain gets the final word.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="page-shell five-questions">
    <div><p class="section-kicker">When you have a transaction digest</p><h2>Ask the same five questions.</h2></div>
    <ol><li>Who acted?</li><li>What went in?</li><li>What ran?</li><li>What changed?</li><li>What did it cost?</li></ol>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
